 ngs and then with validation we found  that 100 of control is passed  uh for the negative controls and then in  comparison with culturing results  we found 90 of the species found by  culturing using ngs  on the bottom here we show the top  antimicrobial resistance genes that we  are found  that we found one of the advantages of  using  ngs is that you don't have to do a lot  of  added tests to profile the microbiome  and then also the drug resistance  so in one test you can actually look for  drug resistance markers and virulence  markers  as well as the microbial profile  so some applications  in the current pandemic  just checking my time here  so applications um with covid19  so as you know coronavirus disease 2019  uh kovid19 has spread causing an  international public health emergency  we're currently almost at we're around  179 000 dead in the u.s alone  there's been an alarming global shortage  of coven 19 diagnostic tests  and almost all of the fda approved tests  currently  are rt pcr-based  so the technology that i was talking  about previously  so this can only um be used to identify  presence in  absence of the virus and doesn't provide  additional insight into the virus  um but you know in this situation we  definitely need to know more about this  virus to stop it  so we need to be able to quantify it and  see how it's evolving  and characterize mutations and strains  so some examples of some work some  studies that are coming out  showing the utility of knowing more  about the virus basically  viral load and disease severity um  have been shown to be correlated in some  samples  in some groups so um when we compare  just the asymptomatic patients to the  patients that have mild symptoms there  doesn't seem to be much of a difference  um but when researchers look at  mild cases uh compared to severe cases  as uh shown here this is some data from  zheng at all  it we see a significant difference  with higher viral loads for more severe  cases  in another study by payatas  there were a number of 1 145 patients  um were tested and it was found that  viral load  um was correlated with higher mortality  so there is some relevance here to  quantify  um level of the virus  and then similarly um it's important to  get an idea of the sequence of the virus  so that we can look at mutations and see  how the virus is evolving  so this here is  occasions i'm going to discuss the  mutations in the spike protein  so the spike protein is a trimeric  transmembrane glycoprotein complex  um it protrudes outward on the surface  of the virus so it's shown in blue  here on the right in the diagram  and so it really gives coronavirus it's  its name in that characteristic shape  uh the spike protein binds to the ace2  receptors in the host  and it's responsible for viral entry  into host cells  so we are seeing emergence of this  important  mutation in the spike protein which is  d614g  mutation and studies are showing that  this  d614 mutation d614g mutation  can increase the binding efficiency to  ace2  and it also may interact with the host  immune system making the infection more  acute  and may make the virus more  transmissible and pathogenic  so this is really the first mutation  that has  received a bit of  indication that it might have clinical  relevance but we also expect that as  the virus spreads and evolves  that we will see other mutations  emerging that will be important as well  so these mutations are good to note um  for how  we both both for treatment of the virus  and then containment of the virus  so we've been um working a bit on using  ngs for covid  for for the virus sarsko v2 which causes  covid19  um and we have been seeing  this ngs approaches providing these  other insights into the virus so  quantification  uh full viral sequence which allows us  to look at mutations  and uh can be used for surveillance of  the  you know entire stars cov2 viral genome  um so one of the things one of the tests  we're running is a  target enrichment of the virus from  samples  because the virus exists at really low  levels in samples  um and get swamped out like the signal  gets swamped out  because the human genome is so big when  you sequence it  so we've been doing this sort of um  hybrid capture  target enrichment for the total viral  genome  um and we're seeing very good coverage  when we do that so on the top  here these um this blue sort of  landscape or mountains  uh shows the sequence uh coverage  across the total viral genome the total  30 000 base pairs  so we see coverage across the the total  genome and then the red lines  here show different genetic variants  in a specific test sample and then on  the bottom here  we see this is compared to the global  database  so researchers have been collaborating  and  uh sequencing this is more than four  thousand samples  that have been deposited into this  database  of the gis aid database and this allows  us to  look in different regions of the world  and see  we can classify different clades of the  virus and different strains that are  merging  and track these key mutations like d614g  so of course there are still challenges  around ngs  and then there's a lot of exciting work  uh that we're starting to do and that we  see in the future  so some of the challenges are that there  are there is still a need for  standardization  before wider adoption in the clinic  so tests need to be reproducible across  time and space  if you have a patient that's tested in  new york in april  you need to be able to be able to  compare results  if they're retested in california six  months later  so one group working on this is the  international metagenomics microbiome  standards alliance  or imsa and our co-founder chris mason  has been working with them on this  and um they're doing a lot of comparison  between testing tools and kind of  benchmarking testing tools  there's a number of companies that are  building  um lab tools uh  like positive controls and stuff that we  can use  so zymo um has a microbial community  that you can always run and you know  uh how much of in your positive sample  how much of  x number of species uh you should be  finding  so these are generation of these  microbial standards and then all of this  feeds into  uh regulatory challenges right so  currently  the fda um has not approved any  meta genomic test which uses ngs to you  know profile the total microbiome  there's none of those tests that have  been uh fda approved  however there has been some changes  recently  the fda has approved approved a couple  tests  for emergency use authorization to use  ngs  uh to detect covid to detect sars  kobichu  um some other challenges are the high  rates of false positives  so this is when you have software that's  really using  a small segment of the genome to  identify a species  and oftentimes you have conservation or  conserved regions between  different species so  software might misidentify different  pathogens or microbial species  and there are ways to address this  computationally  you can incorporate looking at  more coverage across the genome is one  example something that we're working on  another problem on the clinical side  is that this sort of technology has a  really high sensitivity  and you're reporting a lot of pathogens  and sometimes clinicians might not want  to actually  see some of the pathogens that you're  reporting so  an example of this is uh c diff so  c diff um can be found um  carried by patients it can colonize  patients  but that doesn't necessarily mean that  there is a  c diff infection that needs to be  treated  so it's difficult for uh clinicians to  receive a seed of positive report and  then know what to do with that  so there's ways to address this by  looking  at basically one way is using artificial  intelligence to try to differentiate  between  what colonization looks like versus what  an infection that requires treatment  looks like  and then this requires of course more  better better data  and clinical metadata  so a little bit about the future  i'm just checking the time here okay i  think we're good  so a little bit more just about the  future of ngs and infectious disease  so we are already using ngs to profile  the microbiome  of whole cities internationally um this  is some  work that's being done by the medisub  group  um which again is is led by our  co-founder chris mason and we're  involved in a bit  um this gives us a really interesting  um baseline for cities and also we're  seeing is really important when you have  a pandemic  hit and you can um start to know what  your baseline is and  maybe you can use ngs for early  detection and studies and that sort of  thing  [Music]  um we've also been using to describe  using ngs to describe  novel strains of uh different species  collected from the space station  so we've been working with nasa on this  and we have a  couple papers coming out on that  and then ngs has really been providing  extraordinary insight into coven 19 and  you can look at next stream which is  available online which you can see the  spread of the pathogen globally and how  it's evolving  these different strains emerging it's  really amazing tool  and that is leveraging data again from  gis aid  so it's really an incredible example  of researchers working collaboratively  internationally  and making an incredible amount of  progress  um on this problem um so  the goal of course is to enable  personalized data-driven  disease management at all levels from  the individual to the society  at the individual level we want to  provide rapid insight for personalized  treatment to save each life  at the community level we want all these  data to feed into  a database like gis aid  for predictive analytics to drive  community health decisions  to aid in the development of  therapeutics and prediction  to contain emerging outbreaks and  ultimately we want to preserve the  efficacy of therapeutics  and inform the development of new  therapeutics including vaccines  and then preserve the shelf life of  antimicrobials  through judicious use of antimicrobials  and all of this is working towards  eradicating death due to infectious  disease  so i'd like to thank the bioshock team  including chris mason  uh dorati anaj zakal who's our chief  medical officer  at bioshock maryland mara  courtney heather joe all of the folks  working on this work  and then also the infectious disease  team at wow cornell  mason lab at weill cornell belmont grad  international hospital  lenko labs and the university of  tennessee health science center  and thanks for watching
 

